1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,780
 So let's talk about generative AI.

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:04,740
 Just before we go into Amazon Bedrock,

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:07,300
 which is a service for JNAI on AWS,

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,660
 but I first want to take a step back and just understand what is JNAI.

5
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:14,940
 So generative JI or JNAI is going to be a subset of deep learning,

6
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:18,420
 which is a subset of machine learning, which is a subset of AI.

7
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:21,020
 So JNAI has the name indicates,

8
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:24,620
 is used to generate new data that is going to be

9
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:27,020
 similar to the data it was trained on.

10
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,020
 So what type of data can we train JNAI with?

11
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,800
 Well, we can train it on text,

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,540
 on images, on audio, on code,

13
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:37,740
 and video, and a lot more, whatever you think of really.

14
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:41,980
 So the generative AI model is going to take a lot of train data.

15
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:46,780
 Here's an example where we are going to give it a lot of dogs images.

16
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,620
 So we have our train data set which are going to be dogs,

17
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,820
 but also we're going to feed them cartoons.

18
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:56,420
 So we're going to have a bunch of different cartoons that are going to be hand-drawn.

19
00:00:56,420 --> 00:01:01,460
 And the generative model is going to see so many of these train data.

20
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:05,000
 That is going to understand what is a dog, what is a cartoon.

21
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,340
 And then if we ask it, can you generate a cartoon dog?

22
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:13,900
 It's going to be smart enough to combine the two together and create a dog that looks like a cartoon.

23
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:16,780
 And that is the whole power of generative AI,

24
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:21,660
 is that it's able to combine its knowledge into new and unique ways.

25
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:25,900
 So we're going to start with a lot of unlabeled data,

26
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,420
 and we'll see that means in the later section.

27
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:31,620
 And we're going to train what's called a foundation model.

28
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:35,820
 And foundation model are very broad, they're very big, very wide,

29
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:39,300
 and they can adapt to different kinds of general tasks.

30
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:43,140
 For example, a good foundation model can generate some text,

31
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:46,500
 can summarize some text, can extract information,

32
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,460
 can generate images, can become a chatbot,

33
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:52,460
 and can insert any types of questions you have.

34
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:57,260
 So as a whole, we feel a lot of data into a foundational model,

35
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:00,900
 which has the option to do a lot of different tasks.

36
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:04,860
 So now let's talk about foundation models.

37
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:08,620
 So in order to generate data, as we said, we need to have a foundation model,

38
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:11,660
 and they're trained on variety of inputs.

39
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,700
 But to let you know how big these models are,

40
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,540
 usually to train a foundation model, a good one,

41
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:23,380
 it may cost tens of millions of dollars to train because it is very computational heavy.

42
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:26,740
 It takes a lot of time to train it and a lot of data.

43
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:32,380
 So only a few big companies usually are creating their own foundation model.

44
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:33,620
 So here is an example.

45
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:38,500
 So we have GPT40, that is the name of the foundation model,

46
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:43,420
 behind chat GPT, which is the application where you can chat with an AI.

47
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:48,020
 But there is a wide selection of foundation models from different companies.

48
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:53,260
 So we have OpenAI, this is a company behind chat GPT and GPT40, for example.

49
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,780
 We have meta, so this is the new company behind Facebook.

50
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:01,740
 We have Amazon, Google, and Anthropic, and of course a lot more.

51
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:08,700
 But these companies are pretty big or they have a lot of money to invest into building these foundation models.

52
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,940
 So some of these models are going to be open source.

53
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:15,740
 For example, the GPT40, for example, meta is working a lot on open source models.

54
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:20,860
 We have Google BERT as well, which was one of the first models in the GNAI space,

55
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:22,420
 which also is open source.

56
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:25,220
 But some are under a commercial license.

57
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:30,980
 For example, OpenAI, you have to pay to use GPT at Eastern level, anthropic, and so on.

58
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:35,300
 And so we're going to see how we can access these models on AWS as well.

59
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:40,620
 So next, after the foundation model, we have the large language models or LLM.

60
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:46,060
 So LLMs are a type of AI that is relying again on a foundation model.

61
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:49,660
 But designed to generate coherent human-like texts.

62
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:52,940
 So you've been exposed to LLMs a lot before.

63
00:03:52,940 --> 00:04:01,020
 One of these is chat GPT, the GPT40 type of foundation model and LLM from the company, OpenAI.

64
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:05,980
 So I went to chat GPT and I asked it, "Are you an LLM?"

65
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:09,500
 And it replied, "Yes, I am a large language model developed by OpenAI.

66
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:13,820
 And I can understand and generate human-like texts based on the input I receive."

67
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:20,860
 So this was text that looks like a human-rotate that was answered by GPT when I asked it, "Are you an LLM?"

68
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:26,780
 So the way LLM works is that they're trained on very large amount of text data.

69
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:32,300
 So they're usually very, very, very big models, very heavy, very computational, heavy to use.

70
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,220
 We're talking about billions of parameters.

71
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:40,620
 They're trained on a lot of books, articles, websites, data, or any other type of text data

72
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:44,300
 that is deemed good enough for the LLM training.

73
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:51,660
 So it can perform any wide range of language-related tasks, such as translation, summarization,

74
00:04:51,660 --> 00:04:54,860
 question answering, content creation, and so on.

75
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:58,540
 So how does it work to use an LLM?

76
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:04,700
 So for this, we give it a prompt. So the prompt is a question, a bunch of texts that you're going

77
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:10,220
 to send to the GenAI model, the LLM. For example, what is AWS? That's the prompt.

78
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:15,100
 And we'll have a whole section in this course to understanding how to create a good prompt.

79
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:20,300
 Then the model is going to leverage all the existing content it has internally,

80
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:24,540
 learned from, and then it's going to look at the prompt and answer it.

81
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:29,180
 And so when I asked your GPT, what is AWS? I get the answer.

82
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:33,340
 AWS is a comprehensive cloud computing platform provided by Amazon,

83
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:35,260
 and you can read the rest. It's a long answer.

84
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:38,940
 But you have to do something, and it's a term you have to learn,

85
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:44,300
 which is that the output, the generated text, is non-deterministic.

86
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:48,460
 That means that for every user that is using the same prompt,

87
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:52,540
 you will not necessarily and usually not get the same answer.

88
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,100
 So I went to second time and opened a new chat window and asked,

89
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:00,940
 what is it, AWS? And if you take some time to read this answer,

90
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:05,180
 this was a GPT, you will see that while the answers are similar,

91
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:10,860
 and they explain the same thing pretty much, they are not the same exact answers.

92
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:12,700
 And so this is why it's non-deterministic.

93
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:17,500
 So let's understand why though it is non-deterministic.

94
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:23,340
 So let's take a sentence that is going to be outputted by a LLM.

95
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:26,380
 And the sentence is after the rain, this treats were.

96
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:32,780
 And what's going to happen is that the LLM is going to generate a list of potential words

97
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:39,100
 with probabilities. So what is the next word that is going to be probably here in this sentence?

98
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,060
 So the general model is going to think, and it's going to say, okay,

99
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:47,340
 maybe it's wet, and there's a 0.4 out of one chance that is going to be wet.

100
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:55,500
 Or flooded, 0.25, slippery, 0.15, empty, muddy, clean, blocked, and so on.

101
00:06:55,500 --> 00:07:00,620
 So all these sentences make sense, but there are probabilities.

102
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:05,580
 That means that some of these words are more likely to be the next word in that sentence.

103
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:10,300
 And an algorithm is going to, of course, compute these probabilities.

104
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:15,500
 And another one is going to select a word from that list based on the probabilities.

105
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:19,180
 And for example, we're going to choose the word flooded.

106
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:23,020
 So it's going to be after the rain, the streets were flooded.

107
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:25,260
 And this is all done by the Jenny I model.

108
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:28,220
 So now we have after the rain, the streets were flooded.

109
00:07:28,220 --> 00:07:31,820
 And the same process happens over and over again.

110
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:36,780
 So what is the next word where it could be end with, but from until,

111
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:38,540
 because and even a dot.

112
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:42,780
 So after the rain, the streets were flooded, dot, and that's the end of the sentence.

113
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:46,380
 So all of these things again have associated probabilities.

114
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:51,020
 And then the next word is going to be selected based on these probabilities.

115
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:56,620
 So this is why when you ask the AI twice the same prompt, you may not get the same answer.

116
00:07:56,620 --> 00:08:01,100
 It's because the sentence is determined, thanks to statistical methods,

117
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:03,260
 and not with deterministic methods.

118
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:07,260
 So that's for LLMs, but let's talk about images as well.

119
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:11,980
 So Gen AI for images works in a way that, for example, you can give it a prompt.

120
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:17,260
 For example, generate a blue sky with white clouds and the word hello written in the sky.

121
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:21,260
 And the Gen AI model is going to actually give you that image.

122
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:23,100
 That's of course we generated for this course.

123
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:27,900
 We can also have images generated from images.

124
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:30,460
 So here we give an image of someone playing piano.

125
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:34,940
 And we're saying transform this image in a Japanese anime style.

126
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:38,140
 And the outcome image is going to be something similar.

127
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:41,180
 But now it looks like it comes out of the manga of a manga.

128
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:44,860
 And then we can also generate texts from images.

129
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:48,220
 So we give it a prompt and say how many apples do you see in the picture?

130
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:51,340
 And we give it a picture with one orange and an apple.

131
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:56,780
 And then the Gen AI is going to look at the image and say, well,

132
00:08:56,780 --> 00:09:00,380
 the picture shows one apple and the other fruit is an orange.

133
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:03,740
 So just to tell you how that works for Gen AI for images.

134
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:07,500
 So you get an idea of how something can generate an image.

135
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:11,340
 There's different methods of course, but one of the popular one nowadays

136
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:13,740
 is the diffusion model from a company.

137
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,380
 For example, named table diffusion, which is using that model heavily.

138
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:20,060
 So let's take a picture and this is a picture of a cat.

139
00:09:20,060 --> 00:09:25,260
 And we're going to do what's called a forward diffusion process.

140
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:29,340
 That means that we're going to add some noise to the image over time.

141
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:33,100
 So this is with a little bit of noise, but it's the same image with a bit of noise.

142
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:34,700
 And then we add more noise.

143
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:36,940
 We can barely recognize the cats.

144
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:41,020
 And then we add more noise and it looks like the cat is entirely gone.

145
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:42,300
 And all we get is noise.

146
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:45,740
 And so we do this for a lot of pictures.

147
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:47,660
 And this is called the forward diffusion process.

148
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:53,020
 And once the algorithm is trained to take images and create noise out of it,

149
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,460
 we do the opposite.

150
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:58,460
 So when we want to generate an image, we're going to start with noise,

151
00:09:58,460 --> 00:10:03,100
 and we're also going to give it a prompt and say, we want a cat with a computer.

152
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:04,860
 And it's called reverse diffusion.

153
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:08,940
 So now that the algorithm has seen how to go from an image to noise,

154
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:10,780
 it will go from noise to image.

155
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:12,860
 So we give it some noise randomly.

156
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:17,660
 And then it says, OK, I'm going to denoise it and it's going to start to look like a cat.

157
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:20,780
 And then denoise it again and then denoise it again.

158
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:23,020
 And then we have the cats with a computer.

159
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:26,780
 So imagine this is a new image generated by the AI and not the exact same.

160
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:28,300
 It was trained on, of course.

161
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:31,740
 So this is how Jenny I works for text for images.

162
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:37,020
 Just remember the concept of an LLM, remember the concept that it's non deterministic.

163
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:41,420
 But now you have a general overview of Jenny I, and I hope you liked it.

164
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:43,580
 And I will see you in the next lecture.

